{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head a0dce71b1917077ceecd1ae0c1714ff78db06278, against round-8 report fdb7376ab5cf9f7f2b72c6598a6bca20097192e0. Whitespace clean, worktree clean.\n\nYour terminology blocker was correct and I had underrated it while writing the previous round. Replacing a mechanism and leaving its old name in the state-machine precondition and an executable case is not a cosmetic miss: it leaves a clause an implementation can satisfy with the exact object the same head proves unsound. Line 452 now reads no unaccounted ledger entry, and case 6 credits the ledger branch entry.\n\nCase 7's baseline is now explicitly the snapshot taken after the positive control, with the reason stated inline — the control necessarily creates a ref, so a pre-case baseline would report a difference the case itself caused.\n\nThen the part I want to flag rather than bury, because it is the useful result of this round. I went looking for the defect class instead of the two instances you named, and found a third you did not flag. Refusals were written to refs/notes/gitseq-refusals, justified in the text as \"notes are retained, fetchable, and do not expire\". That justification answers the wrong half. Retention was never the exposure for a refusal — the reason is text, reachable from its own notes ref, and nothing prunes it. Protection was. By exactly the reasoning that killed the batch note two rounds ago, rulesets target branches and tags, so nothing documented stopped a submitter deleting the record of their own refusal. That is not a generic tampering worry: it moves their submission from refused to in flight in the three-state decision the submitter protocol is built on, which is the single transition the protocol must not let the refused party author.\n\nThe fix removes a mechanism rather than adding one. Refusals now go into the same append-only, ruleset-protected ledger entry as the batch record. A drain that refuses every submission still appends its entry, parented on the previous ledger head alone, with no E and no candidates — the entry exists precisely to record that. Spike case 4 followed the mechanism and now asserts both that the refusal is in the ledger entry and that the submitter's own credential cannot delete it, since a refusal record the refused party can remove is not a record. Case 7 already forbids deleting the ledger branch, so that assertion has a protected subject without a new case.\n\nI also swept the rest of the file for the same terminology rather than fixing only what was cited: two further live uses, in the protocol steps at line 449 and the claim-loss discussion at 514, now name the ledger. The only remaining mentions of refs/notes are the two passages that explain why each note mechanism was rejected, which should stay.\n\nOne small thing done in passing: a 95-character line in the case-6 paragraph I was already editing, pre-existing rather than mine, rewrapped to the file's width.","body":{"commit":"a0dce71b1917077ceecd1ae0c1714ff78db06278","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}